 just wanted to take a breath and grieve  with my family and I'm not being allowed  to do that by these folks yeah and let's  talk about grant for a second because he  was healthy so what happened to him was  just a fluky kind of thing wasn't it he  was healthy he was a healthy man  completely healthy this is a guy who  worked out with a trainer a couple times  a week would go on the rowing machine a  couple times a week a very very healthy  guy but at the same time he had an  underlying genetic risk factor we were  not aware of there was no reason for us  to test for it until that point which  we've now uncovered during his autopsy  but I think what you're seeing is this  pattern of vaccine Skeptics who are  saying oh look at these young healthy  people it must be coveted vaccines and  that is simply not the case you write  that after Grant's death you swallowed  your grief while protecting his legacy  and trying to end these rumors and you  say that medical information can  re-traumatize families are you prepared  to be re-traumatized by writing this  op-ed because you know they were coming  after you before probably going to come  after you again oh I'm sure they will  and I've already gotten a handful of  those kinds of messages I will say the  vast majority of the Outreach that I've  gotten since the op-ed has been very  positive and supportive but look you  know for me it's really important that  his legacy be protected that he not get  pulled into this anti-vaxxer mess and I  will do whatever I need to do to protect  his memory how do you want him to be  remembered  I want him to be remembered as the kind  generous person that he was he was so  generous to anybody from a student to a  young reporter who was coming up in  sports sports journalism and he used  sports journalism as a tool to advance  social justice whether that was for  women and soccer and basketball or for  LGBT rights during his memorial I just  saw the outpouring of love from people  not jus